I chose a simple linear classifier that primarily uses the velocity sign to decide acceleration: a strong negative weight on velocity for the left action (action 0) and a strong positive weight for the right action (action 2). The biases are set so the car initially pushes left (b0 = 2.0) to build momentum, while the neutral action has zero bias. When the velocity becomes positive, the right action’s score overtakes the left, causing the car to accelerate right toward the goal. Position weights are set to zero to keep the policy simple and focus on velocity, which is the key driver for gaining enough kinetic energy in MountainCar. This configuration stays within the allowed [-6.0, 6.0] range and should allow the car to escape the valley and reach the flag faster than the random policies that all yielded -200.
